# safety property:
invariant [safety_1] (    decision(N1,R1,V1) &
    decision(N2,R2,V2)
) -> V1 = V2

Looking at the counterexample, the problem is clear: `chosenAt(0,0)` and `chosenAt(0,1)` are both true, meaning both value 0 and value 1 appear to have been chosen in round 0. This violates the safety property when both values lead to decisions.

By the quorum intersection axiom, any two quorums must have a common member. So if two different values are both chosen in the same round (via different quorums), there must be a node that voted for both values in that round.

The missing invariant is that **each node can vote for at most one value per round**. This prevents the impossible situation where `chosenAt(R, V1)` and `chosenAt(R, V2)` are both true for `V1 ≠ V2` in the same round.

invariant vote(N, R, V1) & vote(N, R, V2) -> V1 = V2

